.TH SAPHIRA-BRIDGE 1 "2026" "saphira-bridge" "Saphira Linux"
.SH NAME
saphira-bridge \- a tiny userspace Ethernet MAC-learning bridge
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B saphira-bridge
.RB \-c
.I CONFIG
.RB [ \-n
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B saphira-bridge
joins two or more interfaces into one Layer 2 segment. It learns which port each
source hardware address is on, forwards known unicast traffic to that port alone,
floods unknown and group traffic to every port except the one a frame arrived on,
and expires addresses it has not seen for a while.
.PP
Linux retains ownership of ARP, NDP, IP, routing and NAT.
.B saphira-bridge
implements none of them. That is deliberate: a second neighbour resolver would
disagree with the first in ways that are indistinguishable from a genuine
duplicate-address event, and a lab segment needs no gateway. Every stack on the
segment therefore behaves exactly as it would behind a kernel bridge.
.PP
The configuration language is deliberately small: which ports, how long an
address may sit unseen, and how many addresses may be held. Unknown keys are
refused rather than ignored, so that a typo cannot silently leave a port
unbridged.
.SH BOUNDARIES
This is a lab instrument, not a network stack. There is no VLAN support, no
spanning tree, no OpenFlow, no tunnelling, no QoS and no bonding. Those belong to
OVS and to
.BR saphira-network ,
which already exist.
.PP
VLAN-tagged frames are refused and counted rather than handled. A tagged frame's
real destination is not at offset 0 and its real ethertype is not at offset 12,
so forwarding it untagged would hand the next port a frame whose headers mean
something else entirely.
.PP
There is no loop prevention. A source address seen on two ports is counted as a
move and reported, because that is what a loop looks like from inside a bridge,
but nothing is repaired: silently healing a miswired topology hides the mistake
that caused it.
.B THE TOPOLOGY MUST BE A TREE.
.SH FORWARDING RULES
.TP
.B unicast source
Learned against the port it arrived on. A group address as a source teaches the
table nothing, because a table that takes a group's word for who it speaks for can
be fed a forged mapping for a legitimate host. The frame still forwards.
.TP
.B known unicast
Sent to the learned port only.
.TP
.B unknown unicast
Flooded, and counted, so a segment flooding far more than it should is visible
rather than merely slow.
.TP
.B broadcast and multicast
Flooded to every port except the ingress port. Never back out the port a frame
arrived on. Multicast is treated as broadcast: there is no IGMP or MLD snooping.
.TP
.B self-addressed
A frame whose destination is the address it arrived from is dropped and counted
rather than reflected, because reflecting it is how a loop multiplies itself.
.TP
.B 802.1D reserved
.BR 01:80:c2:00:00:00 " through " 01:80:c2:00:00:0f
are neither learnt nor forwarded. A bridge that floods other bridges' control
frames is a bridge that can be steered by them.
.SH ADDRESS TABLE
The table is bounded and ages.
.I age_secs
(default 300, the Linux bridge default) sets how long an address may sit unseen
before it is dropped;
.I max_entries
sets the ceiling. Both are load-bearing: the table is filled from addresses
appearing in incoming frames, so without a bound it is a memory exhaustion target,
and without ageing a lab that recreates its interfaces fills it with dead entries
within a few runs.
.PP
When the table is full, expired entries are reclaimed first. Only if that frees
nothing is the least recently seen address given up, counted as
.BR evicted .
A full table is not by itself a reason to drop a live host.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
.BI port= NAME
An interface to bridge. Repeatable. At least two are required. A repeated name is
refused, because it would make "the port this frame arrived from" ambiguous and
every flood would echo back into the sender.
.TP
.BI age_secs= SECONDS
1 to 86400. Default 300.
.TP
.BI max_entries= COUNT
8 to 65536. Default 4096.
.TP
.BI promiscuous= 0|1
Default 1. A bridge must see frames that are not addressed to it.
.TP
.BI uid= N
.TQ
.BI gid= N
The account to drop to once the ports are open. Default 128.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Dump counters, per port and in total.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload
.IR age_secs .
A reload that would change the port set is refused, because that would drop the
address table and a new packet socket cannot be opened after the privilege drop.
.TP
.B SIGTERM
Shut down and print a final summary.
.SH PRIVILEGE
Promiscuous packet sockets require
.BR CAP_NET_ADMIN ,
so the daemon must start privileged. It drops to
.I uid
immediately afterwards and retains no capability. Packet sockets are already
established at that point, so no further privilege is needed. A reload that needs
a new socket is therefore refused rather than attempted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Clean shutdown, or a successful
.BR \-\-check .
.TP
.B 1
Configuration refused, or a port could not be opened.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/saphira/saphira-bridge.conf
.TP
.I /var/log/saphira-bridge.log
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR saphira-udpdist (1),
.BR saphira-gredist (1),
.BR bridge (8),
.BR ip-link (8)
